{"name":"Grey-Headed Woodpecker Green","hex":"#98916c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"},{"term":"headed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a sheet of paper: having the sender's name, address, etc. preprinted at the top.","Having a head or brain with specified characteristics."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of head"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/headed"},{"term":"woodpecker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any bird of the species-rich family Picidae, with a strong pointed beak suitable for pecking holes in wood.","A Type 92 heavy machine gun."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woodpecker"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grey-headed woodpecker","blocks":[{"text":"The grey-headed woodpecker, also known as the grey-faced woodpecker, is a Eurasian member of the woodpecker family, Picidae. Along with the more commonly found European green woodpecker and the Iberian green woodpecker, it is one of three closely related species found in Europe. Its distribution also stretches across large parts of the central and Eastern Palaearctic, all the way to the Pacific Ocean and south to the Himalaya and the Malay Peninsula."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D1%83_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%97%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%91%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8.jpg/330px-%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D1%83_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%97%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%91%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey-headed_woodpecker","wikidata":"Q201106"},{"title":"Woodpecker","blocks":[{"text":"Woodpeckers are part of the bird family Picidae, which also includes the piculets, wrynecks, and sapsuckers. Members of this family are found worldwide, except for Australia, New Guinea, New Zealand, Madagascar, and the extreme polar regions. Most species live in forests or woodland habitats, although a few species are known that live in treeless areas, such as rocky hillsides and deserts, and the Gila woodpecker specializes in exploiting cacti."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/PileatedWoodpeckerFeedingonTree%2C_crop.jpg/330px-PileatedWoodpeckerFeedingonTree%2C_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodpecker","wikidata":"Q25439"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg/500px-%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg/1280px-%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg/500px-%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D0%94%D1%8F%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8E.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Picus_canus_distr_new.png/500px-Picus_canus_distr_new.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Picus_canus_distr_new.png/1280px-Picus_canus_distr_new.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Picus_canus_distr_new.png/500px-Picus_canus_distr_new.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APicus_canus_distr_new.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org